/*
 * OCR A GCSE Chemistry (J248 / Gateway Science) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * 40 written exam-style questions per topic, varying from 1-mark recall to 6-mark extended.
 * Each question has a markScheme (one (1) per mark) and supports diagram: true for questions
 * where the student is expected to draw a diagram as part of the answer.
 * Questions marked [HT] assess Higher-tier-only (bold) learning outcomes.
 */

const CHEMISTRY_OCR_A_GCSE_PRACTICE = {

  /* ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ C1 Particles */
  'C1': {
    name: 'Particles',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Name the change of state when a liquid turns into a solid.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• Freezing (accept solidifying) (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the arrangement and movement of the particles in a solid.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Particles are close together in a regular arrangement / lattice (1)\n• Particles vibrate about fixed positions (1)` },
      { q: 'Compare the arrangement and movement of particles in a liquid and in a gas.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Liquid: particles are close together / touching (1)\n• Liquid: particles move randomly and slide past each other (1)\n• Gas: particles are far apart (1)\n• Gas: particles move quickly in random directions (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw a diagram to show the arrangement of particles in a liquid. Use circles to represent the particles.', marks: 2, diagram: true, markScheme: `• At least six circles, most touching their neighbours (1)\n• Irregular / random arrangement with no regular pattern (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain, in terms of particles, what happens when a solid melts.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Energy is transferred to the particles / the solid is heated (1)\n• Particles vibrate more / gain kinetic energy (1)\n• Particles overcome some of the forces of attraction and can move past each other (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why a gas can be compressed but a liquid cannot easily be compressed.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Gas particles are far apart / there is lots of space between them (1)\n• Liquid particles are already close together / touching, so there is little space to remove (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe what happens to the particles of a substance when it condenses.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Gas particles lose energy / slow down (1)\n• They move closer together and forces of attraction hold them as a liquid (1)` },
      { q: 'Name the change of state when a solid turns directly into a gas.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• Sublimation (1)` },
      { q: 'State two differences between a physical change and a chemical change.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• In a physical change no new substance is formed; in a chemical change new substances are formed (1)\n• Physical changes are usually easily reversed; chemical changes are usually difficult to reverse (1)` },
      { q: 'Classify each of the following as a physical change or a chemical change: (a) ice melting, (b) magnesium burning, (c) salt dissolving in water.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• (a) Physical (1)\n• (b) Chemical (1)\n• (c) Physical (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain, in terms of particles, why boiling water is a physical change but heating sugar until it turns black is a chemical change.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Boiling: water molecules are unchanged / the same particles are present (1)\n• Only the arrangement and spacing of the molecules changes / forces between molecules are overcome (1)\n• Heating sugar: bonds within the molecules are broken and atoms rearranged (1)\n• New substances (e.g. carbon and water) are formed (1)` },
      { q: 'In the simple particle model, particles are represented as small solid inelastic spheres. Describe three limitations of this model when explaining changes of state. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• It does not show the forces of attraction between the particles (1)\n• It does not take into account the size of the particles / particles are not really solid spheres (1)\n• It does not show the space between the particles accurately / particles are not drawn to scale (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why the particle model, with particles drawn as hard spheres, cannot explain why different substances have different melting points. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• The model does not include forces of attraction between particles (1)\n• Melting point depends on the strength of these forces, which differ between substances (1)` },
      { q: 'Name the scientist who proposed that atoms are tiny solid spheres that cannot be divided.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• John Dalton (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the plum pudding model of the atom.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• A ball / sphere of positive charge (1)\n• With negative electrons embedded in it (1)` },
      { q: 'Name the scientist who discovered the electron and proposed the plum pudding model.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• J. J. Thomson (1)` },
      { q: 'In the gold foil experiment, Geiger and Marsden fired alpha particles at thin gold foil. Describe the three observations and state what each showed about the atom.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): all three observations, each correctly linked to what it shows about the atom.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): at least two observations with some correct conclusions.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): at least one observation or conclusion.\nIndicative content:\n• Most alpha particles passed straight through (1)\n• So most of the atom is empty space (1)\n• Some were deflected through small or large angles (1)\n• So the centre (nucleus) is positively charged and repels the alpha particles (1)\n• A very small number bounced straight back (1)\n• So the mass and positive charge are concentrated in a tiny nucleus (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why the results of the gold foil experiment showed that the plum pudding model was wrong.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• In the plum pudding model the positive charge and mass are spread out through the atom (1)\n• So alpha particles would pass through with little or no deflection (1)\n• Some alpha particles bounced back, which needs a small, dense, positive nucleus (1)` },
      { q: 'State the contribution that Niels Bohr made to the model of the atom.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed shells / energy levels (1)\n• At specific distances from the nucleus (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why scientific models of the atom have changed over time.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• New experimental evidence was found (e.g. discovery of the electron, alpha scattering) (1)\n• The old model could not explain the new evidence so it was modified or replaced (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw a labelled diagram of the nuclear model of an atom of lithium (3 protons, 4 neutrons).', marks: 3, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Small central nucleus labelled, containing 3 protons and 4 neutrons (1)\n• 3 electrons shown outside the nucleus (1)\n• Electrons arranged 2 in the first shell and 1 in the second shell (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the structure of an atom in terms of its nucleus and electrons.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• A small positively charged nucleus at the centre (1)\n• Containing protons and neutrons / most of the mass (1)\n• Surrounded by negatively charged electrons in shells (1)` },
      { q: 'State the typical order of magnitude of the radius of an atom.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• 10⁻¹⁰ m (1)` },
      { q: 'The radius of an atom is about 1 × 10⁻¹⁰ m and the radius of its nucleus is about 1 × 10⁻¹⁴ m. Calculate how many times larger the atom is than the nucleus.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• (1 × 10⁻¹⁰) ÷ (1 × 10⁻¹⁴) (1)\n• = 10 000 / 1 × 10⁴ times (1)` },
      { q: 'A bond length in a hydrogen molecule is 7.4 × 10⁻¹¹ m. State whether this is larger or smaller than a typical atomic radius and give its order of magnitude.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Smaller than a typical atomic radius of 10⁻¹⁰ m (1)\n• Order of magnitude 10⁻¹⁰ m (7.4 × 10⁻¹¹ m ≈ 0.74 × 10⁻¹⁰ m) (1)` },
      { q: 'Complete the table of relative charges and relative masses: proton, neutron, electron.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Proton: charge +1, mass 1 (1)\n• Neutron: charge 0, mass 1 (1)\n• Electron: charge −1, mass very small / about 1/1840 (accept 0.0005) (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why an atom has no overall charge.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• It has equal numbers of protons and electrons (1)\n• The positive charges of the protons cancel the negative charges of the electrons (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why almost all of the mass of an atom is in the nucleus.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Protons and neutrons are in the nucleus and each has a relative mass of 1 (1)\n• Electrons have a very small / negligible mass (1)` },
      { q: 'Define the terms atomic number and mass number.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Atomic number: the number of protons in the nucleus of an atom (1)\n• Mass number: the total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus (1)` },
      { q: 'An atom of potassium is represented as ³⁹₁₉K. Calculate the numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons in this atom.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Protons = 19 (1)\n• Neutrons = 39 − 19 = 20 (1)\n• Electrons = 19 (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons in the ion ³²₁₆S²⁻.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Protons = 16 (1)\n• Neutrons = 32 − 16 = 16 (1)\n• Electrons = 16 + 2 = 18 (1)` },
      { q: 'An ion of aluminium, Al³⁺, has mass number 27 and atomic number 13. State the number of electrons in the ion and explain your answer.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• 10 electrons (1)\n• The atom has 13 electrons and loses 3 to form a 3+ ion (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by an ion.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• An atom or group of atoms with an electrical charge because it has gained or lost electrons (1)` },
      { q: 'A student says that a positive ion forms when an atom gains protons. Explain why the student is wrong.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• The nucleus / number of protons does not change when an ion forms (1)\n• A positive ion forms when an atom loses electrons (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by isotopes.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Atoms of the same element / with the same number of protons (1)\n• But different numbers of neutrons (1)` },
      { q: 'Chlorine has two isotopes, ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl (atomic number 17). Compare the numbers of subatomic particles in atoms of the two isotopes.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Both have 17 protons (1)\n• Both have 17 electrons (1)\n• ³⁵Cl has 18 neutrons and ³⁷Cl has 20 neutrons (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why isotopes of the same element have the same chemical properties.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• They have the same number of electrons / same electron arrangement (1)\n• Chemical reactions involve electrons, not neutrons (1)` },
      { q: 'An atom has 11 protons, 12 neutrons and 11 electrons. Write the symbol for this atom in standard notation, showing its mass number and atomic number.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Element identified as sodium, Na (1)\n• ²³₁₁Na — mass number 23 at top, atomic number 11 at bottom (1)` },
      { q: 'An ion X²⁺ has 18 electrons and 20 neutrons. Identify element X and give its mass number.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Protons = 18 + 2 = 20 (1)\n• X is calcium (Ca) (1)\n• Mass number = 20 + 20 = 40 (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how the model of the atom developed from Dalton to Bohr, including the evidence that led to each change.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a detailed, logically ordered account of at least three models with the evidence that caused each change.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): describes at least two models with some linked evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements about one or two models with little or no evidence.\nIndicative content:\n• Dalton: atoms are tiny solid spheres that cannot be divided; atoms of each element are identical (1)\n• Thomson discovered the electron, so atoms must contain smaller particles (1)\n• Thomson proposed the plum pudding model — a ball of positive charge with electrons embedded in it (1)\n• Geiger and Marsden fired alpha particles at gold foil; most passed through but a few bounced back (1)\n• Rutherford proposed the nuclear model: a tiny, dense, positive nucleus with electrons around it and mostly empty space (1)\n• Bohr suggested electrons orbit in fixed shells / energy levels, which agreed with experimental observations (1)` },
    ]
  },

  /* ═════════════════════════════════════════ C2 Elements, Compounds and Mixtures */
  'C2': {
    name: 'Elements, Compounds and Mixtures',
    questions: [
      { q: 'A carton of orange juice is labelled "pure". Explain the difference between the everyday meaning of "pure" and its scientific meaning.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Everyday: nothing has been added / natural / unadulterated (1)\n• Scientific: a single element or compound, not mixed with any other substance (1)` },
      { q: 'A sample of a solid melts sharply at 122 °C. Another sample of the same substance melts over the range 115–119 °C. Explain which sample is pure.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• The first sample is pure (1)\n• A pure substance melts at a fixed / sharp temperature; impurities lower the melting point and make it melt over a range (1)` },
      { q: 'Define relative formula mass (Mr) and calculate the relative formula mass of calcium carbonate, CaCO₃. (Ar: Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Mr is the sum of the relative atomic masses of all the atoms in the formula (1)\n• 40 + 12 + (3 × 16) (1)\n• = 100 (1)` },
      { q: 'Show that mass is conserved in the reaction 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO by calculating the total relative formula masses of the reactants and products. (Ar: Mg = 24, O = 16)', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Reactants: (2 × 24) + (2 × 16) = 80 (1)\n• Products: 2 × (24 + 16) = 80 (1)\n• Totals are equal, so mass is conserved (1)` },
      { q: 'A compound contains 2.4 g of carbon and 0.6 g of hydrogen. Calculate its empirical formula. (Ar: C = 12, H = 1)', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Moles: C = 2.4 ÷ 12 = 0.2; H = 0.6 ÷ 1 = 0.6 (1)\n• Ratio C : H = 0.2 : 0.6 = 1 : 3 (1)\n• Empirical formula CH₃ (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by a formulation and give two examples.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• A mixture designed as a useful product, with components in measured quantities (1)\n• Example, e.g. an alloy such as brass / steel (1)\n• Second example, e.g. paint, medicines, fertiliser, cleaning products, food (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to obtain pure dry crystals of copper sulfate from a mixture of copper sulfate and sand.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Add water and stir / warm to dissolve the copper sulfate (1)\n• Filter to remove the sand (residue) (1)\n• Heat the filtrate to evaporate some water until crystals start to form / to the point of crystallisation (1)\n• Leave to cool and crystallise, then filter and dry the crystals between filter paper / in a warm oven (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw a labelled diagram of the apparatus used for simple distillation of salt water.', marks: 4, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Flask containing salt water heated from below (1)\n• Thermometer with its bulb level with the side arm (1)\n• Condenser with cooling water in at the bottom and out at the top (1)\n• Receiving vessel collecting the distillate (pure water) (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why fractional distillation, not simple distillation, is used to separate ethanol (boiling point 78 °C) from water (boiling point 100 °C).', marks: 3, markScheme: `• The boiling points are close together (1)\n• Simple distillation would collect a mixture of both liquids (1)\n• The fractionating column allows repeated condensation and evaporation so the vapour reaching the top is mainly the lower-boiling ethanol (1)` },
      { q: 'Suggest a suitable method to separate each mixture: (a) chalk from water, (b) ethanol from water, (c) the dyes in a sample of ink.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• (a) Filtration (1)\n• (b) Fractional distillation (1)\n• (c) Chromatography (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by the stationary phase and the mobile phase in paper chromatography.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Stationary phase: the paper (water held in the paper) — does not move (1)\n• Mobile phase: the solvent that moves through the paper (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to carry out paper chromatography to find out which dyes are present in an unknown ink.', marks: 6, diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a clear, logically sequenced method that would give valid results, including how the chromatogram is used to identify the dyes.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): a method with some key steps and some reference to comparing spots.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple steps with little detail.\nIndicative content:\n• Draw a pencil start line about 1–2 cm from the bottom of the chromatography paper (1)\n• Put a small spot of the unknown ink and spots of known dyes on the line (1)\n• Place the paper in a beaker of solvent with the solvent level below the start line (1)\n• Cover the beaker with a lid and leave the solvent to rise up the paper (1)\n• Remove the paper before the solvent reaches the top, mark the solvent front and let it dry (1)\n• Compare the spots / Rf values of the unknown with those of the known dyes; matching spots show the same dye (1)` },
      { q: 'On a chromatogram, the solvent front moved 8.0 cm and a spot moved 3.2 cm. Calculate the Rf value of the spot.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 (1)\n• = 0.40 (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how chromatography can be used to show whether a substance is pure.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• A pure substance produces only one spot (in any solvent) (1)\n• An impure substance / mixture produces two or more spots (1)` },
      { q: 'Thin layer chromatography (TLC) is used to analyse a colourless mixture of amino acids. Suggest how the spots can be seen, and give one advantage of TLC over paper chromatography.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Use a locating agent (e.g. ninhydrin) / view under UV light (1)\n• TLC is faster / gives better separation / needs smaller samples (1)` },
      { q: 'Name a type of chromatography that uses a gas as the mobile phase, and give one use of it.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Gas chromatography (GC) (1)\n• e.g. detecting drugs in blood / testing for alcohol / analysing pollutants / checking purity (1)` },
      { q: 'Compare the physical properties of metals and non-metals.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Metals are good conductors of electricity and heat; non-metals are generally poor conductors (1)\n• Metals are shiny; non-metals are usually dull (1)\n• Metals are malleable / ductile; solid non-metals are brittle (1)\n• Metals usually have high melting points / are solid at room temperature; many non-metals have low melting points (1)` },
      { q: 'An element has the electronic structure 2.8.6. State its group and period and identify the element.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Group 6 (1)\n• Period 3 (1)\n• Sulfur (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how the position of an element in the Periodic Table is related to the arrangement of electrons in its atoms.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Elements are arranged in order of atomic number (number of protons / electrons) (1)\n• The period number equals the number of occupied electron shells (1)\n• The group number equals the number of electrons in the outer shell (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw a dot and cross diagram to show the bonding in sodium chloride. Show the outer shells only.', marks: 3, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Na⁺ shown with no outer electrons / full outer shell of 8 (as a bracketed ion) (1)\n• Cl⁻ shown with 8 outer electrons: 7 dots and 1 cross (1)\n• Charges + and − shown on the correct ions, in square brackets (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw a dot and cross diagram for a molecule of water, H₂O. Show the outer electrons only.', marks: 2, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Two shared pairs, each one dot and one cross, between O and each H (1)\n• Two lone pairs on oxygen, giving oxygen 8 outer electrons (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how an ionic bond forms between magnesium and oxygen.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Magnesium atoms lose / transfer two electrons (1)\n• Oxygen atoms gain two electrons (1)\n• Forming Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ ions with full outer shells (1)\n• The oppositely charged ions are held by strong electrostatic attraction (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the bonding in a metal.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• A lattice / regular arrangement of positive metal ions (1)\n• Surrounded by a sea of delocalised electrons (1)\n• Held by strong electrostatic attraction between the ions and the delocalised electrons (1)` },
      { q: 'Give two limitations of dot and cross diagrams and one limitation of ball and stick models.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Dot and cross diagrams do not show the 3D shape of the substance (1)\n• Dot and cross diagrams suggest electrons from different atoms are different / do not show relative sizes of atoms (1)\n• Ball and stick models show bonds as solid sticks / show gaps between atoms that do not exist (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how the reactions of elements are related to the arrangement of electrons in their atoms.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Atoms react to gain a full / stable outer shell (1)\n• Elements with the same number of outer electrons (same group) react in similar ways (1)\n• Group 0 elements have full outer shells so are unreactive (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how Mendeleev arranged the elements and how his arrangement was refined to give the modern Periodic Table.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Mendeleev arranged elements in order of atomic mass (1)\n• He grouped elements with similar properties and left gaps for undiscovered elements / swapped some elements to fit patterns (1)\n• After protons were discovered, elements were arranged by atomic number (1)\n• This explained the swapped pairs (e.g. Te and I) because isotopes make atomic mass order unreliable (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why there is a very large number of different carbon compounds.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Carbon can form four covalent bonds (1)\n• Carbon atoms can bond to each other to form chains and rings / families of similar compounds (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why diamond is very hard and has a very high melting point.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Each carbon atom is covalently bonded to four other carbon atoms (1)\n• In a giant covalent lattice (1)\n• Many strong covalent bonds must be broken, needing lots of energy (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why graphite conducts electricity and is soft and slippery.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds, in hexagonal layers (1)\n• One electron per carbon atom is delocalised and can move through the structure, so graphite conducts (1)\n• Weak forces between the layers (1)\n• So the layers can slide over each other (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the structures of graphene and of fullerenes, and give one use of fullerenes.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Graphene: a single layer of graphite, one atom thick, of carbon atoms bonded in hexagons (1)\n• Graphene is very strong and conducts electricity (delocalised electrons) (1)\n• Fullerenes: hollow cage / tube molecules of carbon atoms containing rings of five and six atoms, e.g. C₆₀ (1)\n• Use of fullerenes, e.g. drug delivery / lubricants / catalysts / nanotubes to reinforce materials (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain, in terms of bonds and intermolecular forces, why oxygen has a low boiling point.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Oxygen is a simple molecular substance (1)\n• Boiling only needs the weak intermolecular forces between molecules to be overcome (1)\n• Little energy is needed; the strong covalent bonds within molecules are not broken (1)` },
      { q: 'Bromine melts at −7 °C and boils at 59 °C. Predict its state at 20 °C and at 80 °C.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• 20 °C: liquid (1)\n• 80 °C: gas (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why metals are malleable and good conductors of electricity.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Layers of positive ions can slide over each other (1)\n• The delocalised electrons still hold the ions together, so the metal changes shape without breaking (1)\n• Delocalised electrons are free to move through the structure and carry charge (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why poly(ethene) softens on heating at a lower temperature than diamond melts.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Poly(ethene) consists of long chain molecules (1)\n• The forces between the chains are relatively weak intermolecular forces and are overcome at low temperature (1)\n• Melting diamond requires breaking many strong covalent bonds (1)` },
      { q: 'A student says that a copper atom is shiny and conducts electricity. Explain why this statement is incorrect.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Bulk properties belong to the material / many atoms together, not individual atoms (1)\n• Conductivity arises from the delocalised electrons across the metallic lattice (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain the differences in melting point, electrical conductivity and solubility in water of sodium chloride, methane and diamond in terms of structure and bonding.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): explains all three properties for all three substances, clearly linked to structure and bonding.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): explains at least two properties for at least two substances with some reference to bonding.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): describes some properties with limited explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Sodium chloride is a giant ionic lattice with strong electrostatic forces between ions, so it has a high melting point (1)\n• Sodium chloride conducts only when molten or dissolved, when its ions are free to move; it dissolves in water (1)\n• Methane is a simple molecule with weak intermolecular forces, so it has a very low melting point (1)\n• Methane has no ions or delocalised electrons, so it does not conduct; it is insoluble in water (1)\n• Diamond is a giant covalent structure with many strong covalent bonds, so it has a very high melting point (1)\n• Diamond does not conduct (no delocalised electrons) and is insoluble in water (1)` },
      { q: 'State the size range of nanoparticles and compare it with the size of an atom.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Nanoparticles are 1–100 nm (1 × 10⁻⁹ to 1 × 10⁻⁷ m) in size (1)\n• Atoms are about 0.1 nm (10⁻¹⁰ m), so a nanoparticle contains a few hundred to many thousands of atoms / is 10 to 1000 times bigger (1)` },
      { q: 'A cube has sides of 2 cm. Calculate its surface area to volume ratio. Then state how the ratio changes if the side length is reduced to 1 cm.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Surface area = 6 × 2² = 24 cm² (1)\n• Volume = 2³ = 8 cm³ (1)\n• Ratio = 24 : 8 = 3 : 1 (1)\n• For a 1 cm cube the ratio is 6 : 1, so the ratio increases / doubles as the cube gets smaller (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe two uses of nanoparticles and link each use to a property.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Use, e.g. silver nanoparticles in wound dressings / socks (1)\n• Property: antibacterial (1)\n• Use, e.g. titanium dioxide / zinc oxide nanoparticles in sunscreen (1)\n• Property: absorb / block UV light but are transparent on the skin (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain two possible risks associated with the use of nanoparticles.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• They may be breathed in / absorbed through the skin and enter cells, with unknown health effects (1)\n• They may be released into the environment and harm organisms; long-term effects are not yet known (1)` },
    ]
  },

  /* ═════════════════════════════════════════════════ C3 Chemical Reactions */
  'C3': {
    name: 'Chemical Reactions',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Use the formulae of the ions to write the formula of each compound: (a) calcium chloride (Ca²⁺, Cl⁻), (b) aluminium oxide (Al³⁺, O²⁻), (c) ammonium sulfate (NH₄⁺, SO₄²⁻).', marks: 3, markScheme: `• (a) CaCl₂ (1)\n• (b) Al₂O₃ (1)\n• (c) (NH₄)₂SO₄ (1)` },
      { q: 'Balance the equation: Na + H₂O → NaOH + H₂', marks: 1, markScheme: `• 2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂ (1)` },
      { q: 'Write a balanced symbol equation, with state symbols, for the reaction of magnesium with hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride solution and hydrogen.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Correct formulae: Mg + HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (1)\n• Balanced: Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (1)\n• State symbols: Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g) (1)` },
      { q: 'Write the ionic equation, with state symbols, for the precipitation of silver chloride when silver nitrate solution is added to sodium chloride solution. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl (1)\n• State symbols: Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) (1)` },
      { q: 'Zinc reacts with copper sulfate solution: Zn(s) + CuSO₄(aq) → ZnSO₄(aq) + Cu(s). Write the ionic equation and the two half equations for this reaction. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Ionic equation: Zn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s) (1)\n• Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (1)\n• Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu (1)` },
      { q: 'State the value of the Avogadro constant and what it represents. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• 6.02 × 10²³ (1)\n• The number of particles (atoms, molecules or ions) in one mole of a substance (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the number of moles in 11 g of carbon dioxide, CO₂, and the number of molecules this contains. (Ar: C = 12, O = 16; Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³) [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Mr of CO₂ = 44 (1)\n• Moles = 11 ÷ 44 = 0.25 mol (1)\n• Molecules = 0.25 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 1.51 × 10²³ (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the mass, in grams, of one atom of carbon-12. Give your answer in standard form to 3 significant figures. (Avogadro constant = 6.02 × 10²³) [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Mass = 12 ÷ 6.02 × 10²³ (1)\n• = 1.99 × 10⁻²³ g (1)` },
      { q: 'When magnesium ribbon is heated in an open crucible, the mass of the solid increases. Explain this observation.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Magnesium reacts with oxygen from the air to form magnesium oxide (1)\n• The mass of the oxygen atoms that have combined is added to the solid (1)` },
      { q: 'When calcium carbonate is heated in an open tube its mass decreases. Explain, using the particle model, why this does not break the law of conservation of mass.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Calcium carbonate decomposes to calcium oxide and carbon dioxide (1)\n• Carbon dioxide is a gas whose particles escape from the open tube (1)\n• The atoms are not destroyed; if the gas were collected the total mass would be unchanged (1)` },
      { q: '2.4 g of magnesium reacts with 1.6 g of oxygen, O₂, to form 4.0 g of magnesium oxide. Use these masses to deduce the balanced equation. (Ar: Mg = 24, O = 16) [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Moles Mg = 2.4 ÷ 24 = 0.1 mol (1)\n• Moles O₂ = 1.6 ÷ 32 = 0.05 mol and moles MgO = 4.0 ÷ 40 = 0.1 mol (1)\n• Ratio Mg : O₂ : MgO = 0.1 : 0.05 : 0.1 = 2 : 1 : 2 (1)\n• 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (1)` },
      { q: '6.5 g of zinc is added to 0.15 mol of hydrochloric acid. Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. Identify the limiting reactant and calculate the amount in moles of hydrogen formed. (Ar: Zn = 65) [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Moles Zn = 6.5 ÷ 65 = 0.10 mol (1)\n• 0.10 mol Zn would need 0.20 mol HCl (1)\n• Only 0.15 mol HCl is present, so HCl is the limiting reactant (1)\n• Moles H₂ = 0.15 ÷ 2 = 0.075 mol (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the mass of calcium oxide formed when 25 g of calcium carbonate is heated. CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (Ar: Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16) [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Moles CaCO₃ = 25 ÷ 100 = 0.25 mol (1)\n• Moles CaO = 0.25 mol (1 : 1 ratio) (1)\n• Mass CaO = 0.25 × 56 = 14 g (1)` },
      { q: 'When ammonium nitrate dissolves in water, the temperature falls from 21 °C to 14 °C. State whether the process is exothermic or endothermic and explain your answer.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Endothermic (1)\n• Energy is taken in from the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings falls (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw and label a reaction profile for an exothermic reaction. Label the reactants, products, activation energy and energy change.', marks: 4, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Axes energy (y) against progress of reaction (x); products drawn lower than reactants (1)\n• Curve rising to a peak between reactants and products (1)\n• Activation energy labelled from reactants to the peak (1)\n• Energy change labelled as the difference between reactants and products (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by activation energy.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• The minimum energy needed for particles to react / for a reaction to occur (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain, in terms of bond breaking and bond making, why a reaction is exothermic. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Breaking bonds is endothermic / takes in energy (1)\n• Making bonds is exothermic / releases energy (1)\n• In an exothermic reaction more energy is released making bonds than is taken in breaking bonds (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the energy change for H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. Bond energies (kJ/mol): H–H 436, Cl–Cl 242, H–Cl 431. State whether the reaction is exothermic or endothermic. [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Bonds broken = 436 + 242 = 678 kJ/mol (1)\n• Bonds made = 2 × 431 = 862 kJ/mol (1)\n• Energy change = 678 − 862 = −184 kJ/mol (1)\n• Exothermic, because more energy is released than taken in (1)` },
      { q: 'In the reaction 2CuO + C → 2Cu + CO₂, identify which substance is oxidised and which is reduced, in terms of oxygen.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Carbon is oxidised because it gains oxygen (1)\n• Copper oxide is reduced because it loses oxygen (1)` },
      { q: 'In the reaction Fe₂O₃ + 3CO → 2Fe + 3CO₂, identify the reducing agent and the oxidising agent.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Reducing agent: carbon monoxide (CO) (1)\n• Oxidising agent: iron(III) oxide (Fe₂O₃) (1)` },
      { q: 'Magnesium reacts with chlorine: Mg + Cl₂ → MgCl₂. Explain, in terms of electrons, which species is oxidised and which is reduced. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Magnesium loses two electrons / Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻ (1)\n• So magnesium is oxidised (oxidation is loss of electrons) (1)\n• Chlorine gains electrons / Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻, so chlorine is reduced (1)` },
      { q: 'Name the ions present in all acidic solutions and all alkaline solutions.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Acids: hydrogen ions, H⁺ (1)\n• Alkalis: hydroxide ions, OH⁻ (1)` },
      { q: 'Write the ionic equation for all neutralisation reactions between acids and alkalis in aqueous solution.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq) → H₂O(l) (1)` },
      { q: 'Name the salt formed when: (a) sulfuric acid reacts with copper oxide, (b) nitric acid reacts with potassium hydroxide, (c) hydrochloric acid reacts with calcium carbonate.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• (a) Copper sulfate (1)\n• (b) Potassium nitrate (1)\n• (c) Calcium chloride (1)` },
      { q: 'Write a balanced equation for the reaction of sodium carbonate with hydrochloric acid.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Products sodium chloride, water and carbon dioxide: Na₂CO₃ + HCl → NaCl + H₂O + CO₂ (1)\n• Balanced: Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂O + CO₂ (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to make a pure, dry sample of copper sulfate crystals from copper oxide and dilute sulfuric acid.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a clear, logically ordered method that would produce a pure, dry sample, with reasons for key steps.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): a method with most key steps, though some detail or reasoning is missing.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): some relevant steps but not in a workable order.\nIndicative content:\n• Warm a measured volume of dilute sulfuric acid in a beaker (1)\n• Add copper oxide a spatula at a time, stirring, until no more dissolves / excess solid remains (1)\n• Excess copper oxide ensures all the acid has reacted (1)\n• Filter to remove the excess copper oxide (1)\n• Heat the filtrate gently to evaporate some of the water until crystals start to form, then leave to cool and crystallise (1)\n• Filter off the crystals and dry them between filter paper / in a warm oven (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain the difference between a strong acid and a weak acid, giving an example of each. [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• A strong acid is completely ionised in water (1)\n• Example of strong acid: hydrochloric / sulfuric / nitric acid (1)\n• A weak acid is only partially ionised in water (1)\n• Example of weak acid: ethanoic / citric / carbonic acid (1)` },
      { q: 'A student says "a dilute acid is the same as a weak acid". Explain why the student is wrong. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Dilute refers to concentration: a small amount of acid per unit volume of solution (1)\n• Weak refers to the degree of ionisation: the acid is only partially ionised (1)\n• So a strong acid can be dilute and a weak acid can be concentrated (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how the pH scale shows whether a solution is acidic, neutral or alkaline.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• pH less than 7 is acidic (1)\n• pH 7 is neutral (1)\n• pH greater than 7 is alkaline (1)` },
      { q: 'The pH of a solution of hydrochloric acid changes from 2 to 4 when it is diluted. Calculate the factor by which the hydrogen ion concentration has changed. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Each increase of 1 pH unit means the H⁺ concentration decreases by a factor of 10 (1)\n• Two units: decreased by a factor of 100 (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how the pH changes as sodium hydroxide solution is added gradually to hydrochloric acid until the alkali is in excess. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• pH starts low (about 1) and rises slowly at first (1)\n• pH rises sharply around the neutralisation point / passes through 7 (1)\n• pH then levels off at a high value (about 13) when alkali is in excess (1)` },
      { q: 'Compare the use of universal indicator and a pH meter to measure the pH of a solution.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Universal indicator: add a few drops / dip paper and compare the colour with a colour chart (1)\n• A pH meter / probe is placed in the solution and gives a digital reading (1)\n• The pH meter is more precise / gives readings to decimal places and does not depend on judging a colour (1)` },
      { q: 'State the names given to the positive and negative electrodes in electrolysis, and the ions attracted to each.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Cathode (negative) attracts positive ions / cations (1)\n• Anode (positive) attracts negative ions / anions (1)` },
      { q: 'Predict the products at each electrode when molten sodium chloride is electrolysed with inert electrodes.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Cathode: sodium (1)\n• Anode: chlorine (1)` },
      { q: 'Write the half equations for the reactions at each electrode during the electrolysis of molten lead bromide, PbBr₂. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Cathode: Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb (1)\n• Anode: 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻ (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe and explain the products of the electrolysis of aqueous sodium chloride using inert electrodes.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Ions present: Na⁺, H⁺, Cl⁻ and OH⁻ (1)\n• Hydrogen forms at the cathode because hydrogen is less reactive than sodium (1)\n• Chlorine forms at the anode because chloride ions are present (1)\n• Sodium hydroxide solution remains (1)` },
      { q: 'Aqueous copper sulfate is electrolysed using inert (graphite) electrodes. Name the product at each electrode and describe what you would observe.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Cathode: copper (1)\n• Pink / brown solid coats the cathode (1)\n• Anode: oxygen (1)\n• Bubbles of gas at the anode and the blue colour of the solution fades (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why oxygen, not sulfate, is discharged at the anode in the electrolysis of copper sulfate solution. Include the half equation. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Hydroxide ions (from water) and sulfate ions are both attracted to the anode (1)\n• Hydroxide ions are discharged in preference to sulfate ions (1)\n• 4OH⁻ → O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ (1)` },
      { q: 'Copper sulfate solution is electrolysed using copper electrodes. Describe and explain what happens to the mass of each electrode.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• The anode loses mass (1)\n• Copper atoms at the anode lose electrons and go into solution as Cu²⁺ ions (1)\n• The cathode gains mass (1)\n• Cu²⁺ ions gain electrons at the cathode and are deposited as copper (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw a labelled diagram of the apparatus used to electrolyse sodium chloride solution and collect the gases produced.', marks: 4, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Beaker / cell containing sodium chloride solution with two inert (graphite) electrodes (1)\n• Electrodes connected to a d.c. power supply with anode and cathode labelled (1)\n• Test tubes filled with solution inverted over each electrode to collect the gases (1)\n• Hydrogen labelled at the cathode and chlorine at the anode (1)` },
    ]
  },

  /* ═════════════════════ C4 Predicting and Identifying Reactions and Products */
  'C4': {
    name: 'Predicting and Identifying Reactions and Products',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Give three physical properties of the Group 1 metals.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Soft / can be cut with a knife (1)\n• Low density (lithium, sodium and potassium float on water) (1)\n• Low melting points compared with most metals / shiny when freshly cut / conduct electricity (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe what you would see when a small piece of sodium is added to water containing universal indicator.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Sodium floats and moves around on the surface (1)\n• Fizzes / bubbles of gas, melts into a ball (1)\n• Indicator turns purple / blue as an alkaline solution forms (1)` },
      { q: 'Write a balanced equation, with state symbols, for the reaction of potassium with water.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Products potassium hydroxide and hydrogen: K + H₂O → KOH + H₂ (1)\n• Balanced: 2K + 2H₂O → 2KOH + H₂ (1)\n• State symbols: 2K(s) + 2H₂O(l) → 2KOH(aq) + H₂(g) (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why potassium is more reactive than lithium.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Both have one electron in their outer shell, which is lost when they react (1)\n• Potassium has more shells / its outer electron is further from the nucleus (1)\n• There is more shielding and weaker attraction between the nucleus and the outer electron (1)\n• So the outer electron is lost more easily (1)` },
      { q: 'Rubidium is below potassium in Group 1. Predict two observations when rubidium is added to water, and explain your prediction.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Reacts more vigorously / violently than potassium, may explode (1)\n• Produces hydrogen gas and an alkaline solution (rubidium hydroxide) (1)\n• Reactivity increases down Group 1 because the outer electron is lost more easily (1)` },
      { q: 'Give the colour and state at room temperature of chlorine, bromine and iodine.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Chlorine: pale green / yellow-green gas (1)\n• Bromine: red-brown / orange-brown liquid (1)\n• Iodine: grey / black solid (purple vapour) (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the trend in melting and boiling points down Group 7 and explain it.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Melting and boiling points increase down the group (1)\n• The molecules get larger (1)\n• So the intermolecular forces are stronger and need more energy to overcome (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why chlorine is more reactive than bromine.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Halogens react by gaining one electron to complete their outer shell (1)\n• Chlorine atoms are smaller / have fewer shells (1)\n• The outer shell is closer to the nucleus, with less shielding (1)\n• So there is a stronger attraction for the electron to be gained (1)` },
      { q: 'Astatine is below iodine in Group 7. Predict its state at room temperature and its reactivity compared with iodine.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Solid (1)\n• Less reactive than iodine (1)` },
      { q: 'Chlorine water is added to potassium bromide solution. State what you would observe and write a balanced equation for the reaction.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Solution turns from colourless to orange / yellow-orange (1)\n• Cl₂ + KBr → KCl + Br₂ correct formulae (1)\n• Balanced: Cl₂ + 2KBr → 2KCl + Br₂ (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why there is no reaction when iodine solution is added to potassium chloride solution.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Iodine is less reactive than chlorine (1)\n• A less reactive halogen cannot displace a more reactive halogen from its salt (1)` },
      { q: 'Write the ionic equation for the reaction between chlorine and iodide ions, and identify which species is oxidised. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Cl₂ + 2I⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + I₂ (1)\n• Equation balanced for atoms and charge (1)\n• Iodide ions are oxidised because they lose electrons (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to use displacement reactions to determine the order of reactivity of chlorine, bromine and iodine. Include expected results.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a clear, safe method covering all combinations, with correct expected results and a conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): a method with some combinations and some correct results.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements about mixing halogens and salts.\nIndicative content:\n• Add a few drops of each halogen solution (chlorine water, bromine water, iodine solution) to separate solutions of potassium chloride, potassium bromide and potassium iodide in a spotting tile / test tubes (1)\n• Look for a colour change showing that a reaction has occurred (1)\n• Chlorine turns potassium bromide orange and potassium iodide brown — it displaces both (1)\n• Bromine turns potassium iodide brown but does not react with potassium chloride (1)\n• Iodine does not react with potassium chloride or potassium bromide (1)\n• Conclusion: reactivity order chlorine > bromine > iodine; work in a fume cupboard / wear eye protection as halogens are toxic (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why the Group 0 elements are unreactive.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• They have a full outer shell of electrons (1)\n• So they do not easily lose, gain or share electrons (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the trend in boiling points of the Group 0 elements and predict whether xenon is a solid, liquid or gas at room temperature.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Boiling points increase down the group (1)\n• Xenon is a gas at room temperature (1)` },
      { q: 'Give two uses of noble gases, linked to their properties.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Helium in balloons / airships — low density and unreactive (1)\n• Argon in light bulbs / welding — unreactive so prevents the filament / metal oxidising (accept neon in signs — glows when electricity passes) (1)` },
      { q: 'Compare the properties of transition metals with those of Group 1 metals.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Transition metals have higher melting points (1)\n• Transition metals have higher densities / are harder and stronger (1)\n• Transition metals are much less reactive, e.g. with water and oxygen (1)\n• Transition metals form coloured compounds / ions with different charges; Group 1 compounds are white and form only 1+ ions (1)` },
      { q: 'Iron forms two chlorides, FeCl₂ and FeCl₃. Explain what this shows about transition metals, and give one other typical property of their compounds.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Iron forms ions with different charges, Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ (1)\n• Transition metals can form ions with different charges (1)\n• Their compounds are often coloured / they are used as catalysts (1)` },
      { q: 'Give an example of a transition metal or its compound used as a catalyst, and name the process it catalyses.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Iron (1)\n• In the Haber process to make ammonia (accept nickel in hydrogenation of alkenes; vanadium(V) oxide in the Contact process; manganese(IV) oxide in decomposing hydrogen peroxide) (1)` },
      { q: 'Element X is in Group 2 and Period 4. Predict two of its properties and how it reacts with water compared with magnesium.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• It is a metal that forms 2+ ions (1)\n• Forms a basic / ionic oxide, e.g. XO (1)\n• Reacts more vigorously with water than magnesium, as reactivity increases down Group 2 (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how the reactivity of a metal is related to its tendency to form positive ions.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Metals react by losing electrons to form positive ions (1)\n• The more easily a metal forms its positive ion, the more reactive it is (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the reactions, if any, of magnesium, zinc and copper with dilute hydrochloric acid.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Magnesium: fizzes rapidly, giving hydrogen (1)\n• Zinc: fizzes slowly (1)\n• Copper: no reaction (1)` },
      { q: 'A student adds four metals, A, B, C and D, to water and to dilute acid. A reacts vigorously with cold water. B does not react with water but fizzes quickly in acid. C fizzes slowly in acid. D shows no reaction with either. Deduce the order of reactivity.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• A is most reactive and D is least reactive (1)\n• Order A > B > C > D (1)` },
      { q: 'A student puts pieces of metals into solutions of metal salts. Magnesium coats with a brown solid in copper sulfate; zinc turns grey in iron sulfate solution but iron does not react with zinc sulfate. Deduce the order of reactivity of magnesium, copper, zinc and iron as far as the results allow, and explain your reasoning.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from its salt solution (1)\n• Magnesium is more reactive than copper; zinc is more reactive than iron (1)\n• Zinc > iron; magnesium > copper — a further test (e.g. iron in copper sulfate) is needed to link the two pairs (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why displacement of copper by zinc from copper sulfate solution is a redox reaction. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Zinc atoms lose electrons to form Zn²⁺ ions (1)\n• Zinc is oxidised (loss of electrons) (1)\n• Copper ions gain electrons to form copper atoms, so they are reduced (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the test for hydrogen and give the positive result.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Hold a lighted splint at the mouth of the test tube (1)\n• Squeaky pop (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the tests for oxygen and carbon dioxide and give the positive results.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Oxygen: insert a glowing splint (1)\n• It relights (1)\n• Carbon dioxide: bubble the gas through limewater (1)\n• Limewater turns milky / cloudy (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the test for chlorine and give the positive result.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Hold damp (blue) litmus paper in the gas (1)\n• It is bleached / turns white (blue litmus turns red first) (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to carry out a flame test on a solid sample.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Clean a nichrome / platinum wire loop by dipping in concentrated hydrochloric acid and heating until no colour (1)\n• Dip the loop in acid and then into the solid sample (1)\n• Hold the loop in the edge of a blue / roaring Bunsen flame and observe the colour (1)` },
      { q: 'Give the flame test colours for lithium, sodium, potassium, calcium and copper ions.', marks: 5, markScheme: `• Lithium: crimson / red (1)\n• Sodium: yellow / orange (1)\n• Potassium: lilac (1)\n• Calcium: orange-red / brick red (1)\n• Copper: green / blue-green (1)` },
      { q: 'Sodium hydroxide solution is added to solutions containing copper(II), iron(II) and iron(III) ions. Give the colour of each precipitate.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Copper(II): blue precipitate (1)\n• Iron(II): green precipitate (1)\n• Iron(III): orange-brown / red-brown precipitate (1)` },
      { q: 'Calcium ions and zinc ions both give a white precipitate with a few drops of sodium hydroxide solution. Explain how you could tell them apart.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Add excess sodium hydroxide solution (1)\n• The zinc hydroxide precipitate dissolves (forms a colourless solution); the calcium hydroxide precipitate does not (1)` },
      { q: 'Write the ionic equation, with state symbols, for the formation of the precipitate when sodium hydroxide solution is added to copper(II) sulfate solution. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Cu²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(OH)₂ (1)\n• State symbols: Cu²⁺(aq) + 2OH⁻(aq) → Cu(OH)₂(s) (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the test for carbonate ions.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Add dilute acid (e.g. hydrochloric acid) to the sample (1)\n• Effervescence / fizzing occurs (1)\n• The gas turns limewater milky, showing it is carbon dioxide (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to test a solution for halide ions, and give the results for chloride, bromide and iodide.', marks: 5, markScheme: `• Add dilute nitric acid (1)\n• Then add silver nitrate solution (1)\n• Chloride: white precipitate (1)\n• Bromide: cream precipitate (1)\n• Iodide: yellow precipitate (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the test for sulfate ions and explain why the acid is added first.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Add dilute hydrochloric acid followed by barium chloride solution (accept acidified barium nitrate) (1)\n• A white precipitate (of barium sulfate) forms if sulfate is present (1)\n• The acid removes carbonate ions, which would also give a white precipitate (1)` },
      { q: 'A white solid gives a lilac flame. Its solution gives a yellow precipitate with acidified silver nitrate. Identify the compound.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Potassium ion identified from the lilac flame and iodide ion from the yellow precipitate (1)\n• Potassium iodide, KI (1)` },
      { q: 'A green solid dissolves in water. With sodium hydroxide it forms a green precipitate; with dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride it forms a white precipitate. Identify the solid and explain your reasoning.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Green precipitate with sodium hydroxide shows iron(II) ions (1)\n• White precipitate with acidified barium chloride shows sulfate ions (1)\n• The solid is iron(II) sulfate, FeSO₄ (1)` },
      { q: 'Give three advantages of instrumental methods of analysis compared with chemical tests.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• More sensitive — can detect very small amounts of substance (1)\n• More accurate (1)\n• Faster / can be automated (1)` },
      { q: 'A mass spectrum of an unknown compound has peaks at m/z 15, 29, 31 and 46, the last being the peak with the highest m/z. A reference spectrum for ethanol (Mr 46) shows the same pattern. Explain what the mass spectrum tells you about the compound.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• The peak with the highest m/z (the molecular ion peak) gives the relative formula mass (1)\n• So Mr = 46 (1)\n• The other peaks (fragment pattern) match the reference spectrum, so the compound is ethanol (1)` },
    ]
  },

  /* ════════════════════════════════════ C5 Monitoring and Controlling Chemical Reactions */
  'C5': {
    name: 'Monitoring and Controlling Chemical Reactions',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm³, of a solution made by dissolving 4.0 g of sodium hydroxide, NaOH, in water to make 250 cm³ of solution. (Mr NaOH = 40) [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Moles = 4.0 ÷ 40 = 0.10 mol (1)\n• Volume = 250 ÷ 1000 = 0.250 dm³ (1)\n• Concentration = 0.10 ÷ 0.250 = 0.40 mol/dm³ (1)` },
      { q: '10 g of copper sulfate is dissolved to make 500 cm³ of solution. Calculate the concentration in g/dm³. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Volume = 0.500 dm³ (1)\n• Concentration = 10 ÷ 0.500 = 20 g/dm³ (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the mass of sodium chloride needed to make 200 cm³ of a 0.50 mol/dm³ solution. (Mr NaCl = 58.5) [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Moles = 0.50 × 0.200 = 0.10 mol (1)\n• Mass = 0.10 × 58.5 (1)\n• = 5.85 g (accept 5.9 g) (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how the concentration of a solution changes if the same mass of solute is dissolved in double the volume of solution. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Concentration = amount (or mass) of solute ÷ volume of solution (1)\n• Doubling the volume halves the concentration (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to carry out a titration to find the volume of hydrochloric acid needed to neutralise 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide solution.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a detailed, logically ordered method that would give accurate, repeatable results.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): a method with the main steps but lacking some detail on accuracy.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): a few relevant steps with little detail.\nIndicative content:\n• Use a pipette (and filler) to measure 25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide into a conical flask (1)\n• Add a few drops of indicator, e.g. phenolphthalein or methyl orange (1)\n• Fill a burette with hydrochloric acid and record the initial reading at eye level from the bottom of the meniscus (1)\n• Add acid while swirling the flask, adding dropwise near the end point (1)\n• Stop when the indicator just changes colour permanently and record the final reading (1)\n• Repeat until concordant titres (within 0.10 cm³) are obtained and calculate the mean (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why a conical flask, rather than a beaker, is used in a titration, and why a white tile is placed underneath it.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• The flask can be swirled to mix without spilling (1)\n• The white tile makes the colour change of the indicator easier to see (1)` },
      { q: '25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ sodium hydroxide is neutralised by 20.0 cm³ of hydrochloric acid. NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. Calculate the concentration of the acid. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Moles NaOH = 0.100 × 0.0250 = 0.00250 mol (1)\n• Moles HCl = 0.00250 mol (1 : 1 ratio) (1)\n• Concentration HCl = 0.00250 ÷ 0.0200 = 0.125 mol/dm³ (1)` },
      { q: '25.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol/dm³ sodium hydroxide reacts exactly with 12.5 cm³ of sulfuric acid. 2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O. Calculate the concentration of the sulfuric acid in mol/dm³. [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Moles NaOH = 0.200 × 0.0250 = 0.00500 mol (1)\n• Moles H₂SO₄ = 0.00500 ÷ 2 = 0.00250 mol (1)\n• Volume H₂SO₄ = 0.0125 dm³ (1)\n• Concentration = 0.00250 ÷ 0.0125 = 0.200 mol/dm³ (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the volume of 0.0400 mol of carbon dioxide gas at room temperature and pressure. (Molar gas volume = 24 dm³ at RTP) [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Volume = 0.0400 × 24 (1)\n• = 0.96 dm³ (960 cm³) (1)` },
      { q: 'A reaction produces 480 cm³ of hydrogen at room temperature and pressure. Calculate the amount of hydrogen in moles. (Molar gas volume = 24 dm³) [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• 480 cm³ = 0.480 dm³ (1)\n• Moles = 0.480 ÷ 24 = 0.020 mol (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the mass of magnesium needed to produce 1.2 dm³ of hydrogen at RTP. Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ (Ar Mg = 24; molar gas volume = 24 dm³) [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Moles H₂ = 1.2 ÷ 24 = 0.050 mol (1)\n• Moles Mg = 0.050 mol (1 : 1 ratio) (1)\n• Mass Mg = 0.050 × 24 = 1.2 g (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the volume of oxygen needed to burn 30 cm³ of methane completely. CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O (all volumes at the same temperature and pressure) [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Equal volumes of gases contain equal numbers of moles, so the volume ratio CH₄ : O₂ = 1 : 2 (1)\n• Volume of oxygen = 60 cm³ (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the maximum (theoretical) mass of magnesium oxide that can be made from 12 g of magnesium. 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO (Ar: Mg = 24, O = 16)', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Moles Mg = 12 ÷ 24 = 0.50 mol (1)\n• Moles MgO = 0.50 mol (1)\n• Mass MgO = 0.50 × 40 = 20 g (1)` },
      { q: 'A student expected to make 20 g of magnesium oxide but obtained 15 g. Calculate the percentage yield.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Percentage yield = (actual ÷ theoretical) × 100 = (15 ÷ 20) × 100 (1)\n• = 75% (1)` },
      { q: 'Give three reasons why the actual yield of a reaction is usually less than the theoretical yield.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• The reaction may be reversible / not go to completion (1)\n• Some product is lost when it is separated / transferred / filtered (1)\n• Side reactions produce unwanted products (1)` },
      { q: 'Define atom economy.', marks: 1, markScheme: `• (Mr of desired product ÷ total Mr of all reactants) × 100 / the percentage of the mass of reactants that ends up in the desired product (1)` },
      { q: 'Calculate the atom economy for making calcium oxide by heating calcium carbonate. CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (Mr: CaCO₃ = 100, CaO = 56)', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Atom economy = (56 ÷ 100) × 100 (1)\n• = 56% (1)` },
      { q: 'Ethanol can be made by fermentation, C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂, or by hydrating ethene, C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. Calculate the atom economy of each process. (Mr: C₆H₁₂O₆ = 180, C₂H₅OH = 46)', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Fermentation: (2 × 46 ÷ 180) × 100 (1)\n• = 51% (1)\n• Hydration of ethene: 100% as ethanol is the only product (1)` },
      { q: 'Ethanol can be made by fermentation (C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂, atom economy 51%) or by hydration of ethene (C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH, atom economy 100%). Explain why a company might choose fermentation even though it has the lower atom economy. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Fermentation uses renewable raw materials (sugar from crops); ethene comes from crude oil, which is finite (1)\n• Fermentation works at low temperature and pressure so uses less energy (1)\n• The by-product carbon dioxide may be useful / sold, and the choice also depends on rate, yield and cost (1)` },
      { q: 'Suggest three practical methods for following the rate of the reaction between marble chips and hydrochloric acid.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Measure the volume of carbon dioxide produced over time using a gas syringe / upturned measuring cylinder (1)\n• Measure the loss in mass over time with the flask on a balance (1)\n• Time how long it takes for the marble chips to disappear / the reaction to stop (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of reaction between sodium thiosulfate solution and hydrochloric acid using the disappearing cross method.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a detailed, logically ordered method with control variables and how the rate is found.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): a workable method with some control variables or processing.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): some relevant steps with little detail.\nIndicative content:\n• Draw a black cross on paper and place a conical flask on top (1)\n• Warm sodium thiosulfate solution in a water bath to a set temperature and measure it with a thermometer (1)\n• Add a fixed volume of hydrochloric acid and start a stopwatch (1)\n• Stop timing when the cross can no longer be seen through the cloudy (sulfur) mixture (1)\n• Repeat at several different temperatures, keeping volumes and concentrations of both solutions the same (1)\n• Repeat each temperature, calculate a mean time and work out rate as 1/time; plot rate against temperature (1)` },
      { q: 'In a disappearing cross experiment the cross disappeared after 40 s. Calculate the relative rate of reaction as 1/time.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Rate = 1 ÷ 40 (1)\n• = 0.025 s⁻¹ (1)` },
      { q: 'A reaction produced 60 cm³ of gas in the first 30 s. Calculate the mean rate of reaction over this time.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Rate = volume ÷ time = 60 ÷ 30 (1)\n• = 2.0 cm³/s (1)` },
      { q: 'Sketch a graph of the volume of gas produced against time for a reaction. On the same axes, sketch the curve you would expect if the same reaction were done at a higher temperature with the same amounts of reactants.', marks: 4, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Original curve rising steeply at first then levelling off (1)\n• Gradient decreases as reactants are used up (1)\n• Higher-temperature curve steeper at the start (1)\n• Levels off at the same final volume, but sooner (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how the gradient of a volume of gas against time graph shows how the rate of reaction changes during the reaction.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• The gradient (slope) of the curve equals the rate of reaction (1)\n• The gradient is steepest at the start, when the concentration of reactants is highest (1)\n• The gradient decreases to zero as reactants are used up and the reaction stops (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how to find the rate of reaction at a particular time from a curved graph of mass lost against time.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Draw a tangent to the curve at that time (1)\n• Calculate the gradient of the tangent = change in y ÷ change in x (1)\n• Give the rate in units of g/s (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain, in terms of particles, why increasing the concentration of an acid increases the rate of its reaction with magnesium.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• More acid particles in the same volume (1)\n• So more frequent collisions between acid particles and magnesium (1)\n• More successful collisions per second (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain, in terms of particles, why increasing temperature increases the rate of reaction.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Particles gain kinetic energy and move faster (1)\n• They collide more frequently (1)\n• A greater proportion of collisions have energy greater than or equal to the activation energy (1)\n• So there are more successful collisions per second (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why increasing the pressure of a reaction between gases increases the rate of reaction.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• The same number of gas particles occupy a smaller volume / particles are closer together (1)\n• So collisions are more frequent (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why powdered calcium carbonate reacts faster with hydrochloric acid than the same mass of marble chips.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Powder has a larger surface area to volume ratio (1)\n• More particles are exposed to the acid (1)\n• So there are more frequent collisions (1)` },
      { q: 'A cube of a reactant with 1 cm sides is cut into eight cubes with 0.5 cm sides. Calculate the total surface area before and after cutting and explain the effect on the rate of reaction.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Before: 6 × 1 × 1 = 6 cm² (1)\n• After: 8 × 6 × 0.5 × 0.5 = 12 cm² (1)\n• The surface area doubles while the volume is unchanged (1)\n• So collisions are more frequent and the rate increases (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by a catalyst.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• A substance that speeds up a reaction (1)\n• Without being used up / is chemically unchanged at the end (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe an experiment to show that manganese(IV) oxide is a catalyst for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Weigh a sample of manganese(IV) oxide (1)\n• Add it to hydrogen peroxide and compare the rate of oxygen production with hydrogen peroxide alone, e.g. by collecting gas in a gas syringe (1)\n• The rate is much faster with manganese(IV) oxide (1)\n• At the end, filter, dry and reweigh the manganese(IV) oxide to show its mass is unchanged (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw a reaction profile for an exothermic reaction, showing the reaction pathway with and without a catalyst. Explain how the catalyst increases the rate.', marks: 4, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Profile with products lower than reactants and a peak for the uncatalysed reaction (1)\n• Lower peak drawn for the catalysed pathway, with the same reactant and product levels (1)\n• The catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy (1)\n• So a greater proportion of collisions are successful (1)` },
      { q: 'State what enzymes are and give one example of a reaction they catalyse.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Biological catalysts / proteins that act as catalysts in living things (1)\n• e.g. amylase breaking down starch / yeast enzymes in fermentation of glucose (1)` },
      { q: 'Heating blue hydrated copper sulfate gives white anhydrous copper sulfate and water. Describe what happens when water is added to the white solid and what this shows.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• The white solid turns blue and gets hot (1)\n• The reaction is reversible (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain what is meant by dynamic equilibrium.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• In a closed system (1)\n• The rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction (1)\n• The concentrations of reactants and products stay constant (they are not necessarily equal) (1)` },
      { q: 'For the equilibrium N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), the forward reaction is exothermic. Predict and explain the effect on the yield of ammonia of (a) increasing the pressure and (b) increasing the temperature. [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• (a) Yield increases (1)\n• The equilibrium shifts to the side with fewer gas molecules (2 rather than 4) (1)\n• (b) Yield decreases (1)\n• The equilibrium shifts in the endothermic (reverse) direction to oppose the temperature rise (1)` },
      { q: 'For the equilibrium 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), explain the effect of adding more oxygen on the position of equilibrium. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• The equilibrium shifts to the right (1)\n• To oppose the change by using up the added oxygen, so more SO₃ is made (1)` },
      { q: 'Consider the equilibrium H₂(g) + I₂(g) ⇌ 2HI(g). Explain why changing the pressure has no effect on the position of equilibrium. [HT]', marks: 2, markScheme: `• There are equal numbers of gas molecules on each side (2 and 2) (1)\n• So neither direction reduces the pressure / the system cannot oppose the change (1)` },
    ]
  },

  /* ═════════════════════════════════════════════════ C6 Global Challenges */
  'C6': {
    name: 'Global Challenges',
    questions: [
      { q: 'Explain, using the reactivity series, why iron can be extracted from iron oxide by heating with carbon but aluminium cannot be extracted from aluminium oxide in this way.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Carbon is more reactive than iron, so it can remove the oxygen from iron oxide / reduce it (1)\n• Aluminium is more reactive than carbon (1)\n• So carbon cannot reduce aluminium oxide (1)` },
      { q: 'Copper can be extracted by heating copper oxide with carbon. Write a balanced equation for the reaction and state which substance is reduced.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• 2CuO + C → 2Cu + CO₂ correct formulae (1)\n• Correctly balanced (1)\n• Copper oxide is reduced (it loses oxygen) (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why and how electrolysis is used to extract aluminium from aluminium oxide.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Aluminium is more reactive than carbon, so electrolysis must be used (1)\n• Aluminium oxide is dissolved in molten cryolite to lower the melting point / reduce energy costs (1)\n• Aluminium ions are attracted to the cathode, where aluminium forms (1)\n• Oxygen forms at the carbon anode, which reacts to form carbon dioxide so the anodes must be replaced (1)` },
      { q: 'Evaluate the use of phytoextraction and bacterial extraction (bioleaching) to obtain copper from low-grade ores. [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Phytoextraction: plants absorb copper compounds from the soil; the plants are harvested and burned and copper is extracted from the ash (1)\n• Bioleaching: bacteria convert copper compounds in the ore into a solution (leachate) from which copper is extracted (1)\n• Advantages: use low-grade ores / waste heaps, less energy and less mining, less damage to the landscape (1)\n• Disadvantages: very slow; bioleaching can produce toxic leachate (1)` },
      { q: 'The Haber process uses a temperature of about 450 °C. N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ (forward reaction exothermic). Explain why this temperature is a compromise. [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• A lower temperature would give a higher equilibrium yield because the forward reaction is exothermic (1)\n• But at a low temperature the rate would be too slow (1)\n• A higher temperature gives a faster rate but a lower yield (1)\n• 450 °C gives an acceptable yield at an acceptable rate (1)` },
      { q: 'A graph shows that, at 200 atm, the percentage yield of ammonia at equilibrium is about 70% at 300 °C and about 30% at 450 °C. Explain why industry still uses 450 °C, using ideas about rate and the use of an iron catalyst. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• At 300 °C the rate would be very slow, so equilibrium would take a long time to reach (1)\n• Ammonia is produced faster at 450 °C, so more is made per day despite the lower yield (1)\n• The iron catalyst increases the rate further; unreacted gases are recycled so little is wasted (1)` },
      { q: 'The Contact process makes sulfur trioxide: 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ (exothermic). It uses about 450 °C, 1–2 atm and a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst. Explain why such a low pressure is used. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Higher pressure would favour SO₃ as there are fewer gas molecules on the right (1)\n• But the equilibrium yield is already very high (about 99%) at low pressure (1)\n• So the extra cost of high-pressure equipment and energy is not worthwhile (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why the Haber process is important for food production, and name the three elements needed in fertilisers.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Ammonia is used to make nitrogen fertilisers (e.g. ammonium nitrate) (1)\n• Fertilisers replace elements used by crops and increase crop yields, feeding a growing population (1)\n• Nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium (NPK) (1)` },
      { q: 'Compare the industrial production of ammonium sulfate with its preparation in a school laboratory.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Laboratory: ammonia solution is titrated with dilute sulfuric acid, then the solution is crystallised (1)\n• Industry: ammonia and sulfuric acid are made from raw materials (air, natural gas, sulfur) in integrated processes (1)\n• Industry is continuous and on a very large scale; the laboratory is a small-scale batch process (1)\n• Industry uses concentrated reactants and heat released by the reaction; the laboratory uses dilute solutions and needs energy to evaporate water (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how ammonium nitrate fertiliser is produced industrially from raw materials in several integrated processes.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Nitrogen from the air and hydrogen from natural gas (methane) are used to make ammonia by the Haber process (1)\n• Some ammonia is oxidised to make nitric acid (1)\n• Ammonia is then reacted with nitric acid (1)\n• NH₃ + HNO₃ → NH₄NO₃, ammonium nitrate (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the four stages that are considered in a life-cycle assessment (LCA) of a product.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Extracting and processing raw materials, and making the product (1)\n• Using the product (1)\n• Transporting the product (1)\n• Disposal at the end of its life (landfill, incineration or recycling) (1)` },
      { q: 'An LCA shows that a plastic bag uses 1.6 MJ of energy and produces 0.1 kg CO₂, while a paper bag uses 3.6 MJ and produces 0.2 kg CO₂. Interpret these data and suggest one limitation of using them to decide which bag is better.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• The paper bag uses about twice as much energy (1)\n• And produces twice as much CO₂, so on these data the plastic bag has less impact (1)\n• Limitation: data ignore number of reuses / biodegradability / litter / LCA values can involve value judgements (1)` },
      { q: 'Evaluate the factors that affect decisions on whether to recycle a material, using glass and aluminium as examples. Include a process where a material is recycled for a different use.', marks: 6, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a balanced evaluation of several factors with both examples and a reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): describes some factors with at least one example.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements about recycling.\nIndicative content:\n• Recycling saves finite raw materials and reduces waste sent to landfill (1)\n• Recycling aluminium uses far less energy (about 5%) than extracting it from bauxite by electrolysis, so it is very viable (1)\n• Glass can be crushed and melted to make new bottles, or crushed and used for a different purpose such as road surfacing / building aggregate (1)\n• Costs of collection, transport and sorting, and energy used, can make recycling less viable (1)\n• Contamination or mixing of materials (e.g. different coloured glass) reduces the value of recycled material (1)\n• Conclusion: recycling is worthwhile when the energy and money saved exceed the costs of collecting and processing (1)` },
      { q: 'Give the main metals in brass, bronze and solder, and one use of each.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Brass: copper and zinc — e.g. musical instruments, door handles, electrical plugs (1)\n• Bronze: copper and tin — e.g. statues, medals, ship propellers (1)\n• Solder: tin and lead (or tin with copper / silver) — joining electrical components (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why steel and duralumin are more useful than the pure metals iron and aluminium.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Steel is iron with carbon (and sometimes other metals) (1)\n• Duralumin is aluminium with copper (and magnesium) (1)\n• The different sized atoms distort the layers so they cannot slide easily over each other (1)\n• So the alloys are harder / stronger, e.g. duralumin is strong and light for aircraft, steel for buildings (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe an experiment to show that both water and oxygen are needed for iron to rust.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Tube 1: iron nail in tap water open to the air — rusts (1)\n• Tube 2: nail in boiled water with a layer of oil on top (no oxygen) — does not rust (1)\n• Tube 3: nail in dry air with anhydrous calcium chloride to absorb water — does not rust (1)\n• Leave for several days and compare; only the nail with both water and oxygen rusts (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how galvanising protects iron from rusting, even when the zinc coating is scratched.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• The zinc coating acts as a barrier keeping out oxygen and water (1)\n• Zinc is more reactive than iron (1)\n• So if scratched, zinc corrodes / is oxidised instead of iron — sacrificial protection (1)` },
      { q: 'Compare the properties of glass, a clay ceramic, poly(ethene), a carbon-fibre composite and steel, and suggest which material is best for making a lightweight bicycle frame.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Glass and clay ceramics are hard but brittle and have high melting points (1)\n• Poly(ethene) is light and flexible but has low strength and softens at low temperatures (1)\n• Steel is strong and tough but dense (1)\n• Carbon-fibre composite is strong and has a low density, so it is best for a lightweight frame (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by a homologous series and name the functional groups in alkenes, alcohols and carboxylic acids.', marks: 5, markScheme: `• A family of compounds with the same general formula and the same functional group (1)\n• Similar chemical properties, with successive members differing by CH₂ (1)\n• Alkenes: C=C double bond (1)\n• Alcohols: –OH (1)\n• Carboxylic acids: –COOH (1)` },
      { q: 'State what is meant by a homologous series.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• A family of compounds with the same general formula and the same functional group (1)\n• Similar chemical properties, with each member differing by CH₂ / showing a trend in physical properties (1)` },
      { q: 'Write a balanced equation for the complete combustion of propane, C₃H₈.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O correct products (1)\n• C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe a test to distinguish between hexane and hexene, and name the product formed with ethene.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Add bromine water to each (1)\n• Hexene decolourises bromine water (orange to colourless); hexane does not (no change) (1)\n• Ethene forms 1,2-dibromoethane (1)` },
      { q: 'Predict the product when hydrogen is added to propene, and give the conditions.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Propane (1)\n• Nickel catalyst at about 150 °C (1)` },
      { q: 'Ethanol is warmed with acidified potassium manganate(VII). Name the organic product and describe the colour change.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• Ethanoic acid (1)\n• Purple to colourless (1)` },
      { q: 'Draw the repeating unit of poly(chloroethene), PVC, formed from the monomer chloroethene, CH₂=CHCl. Show it in the form [repeat unit]n.', marks: 3, diagram: true, markScheme: `• Two carbon atoms joined by a single bond (1)\n• Three H atoms and one Cl atom on the carbons (1)\n• Extension bonds through square brackets with n outside (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain the basic principles of addition polymerisation.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• The monomers contain a C=C double bond (1)\n• The double bond opens and many monomers join together in long chains (1)\n• No other product is formed; the polymer is the only product (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain how a polyester is formed by condensation polymerisation. You may use block diagrams to represent the monomers. [HT]', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Two different monomers, each with two functional groups, e.g. a diol (HO–□–OH) and a dicarboxylic acid (HOOC–■–COOH) (1)\n• The –OH and –COOH groups react to form an ester link (1)\n• A small molecule (water) is formed for each link made (1)\n• Alternating monomers join to form a long chain: [–O–□–O–CO–■–CO–]n (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how nylon can be made in the laboratory. [HT]', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Pour a solution of a diamine in water into a beaker, then carefully add a solution of a diacid chloride in an organic solvent on top, without mixing (1)\n• Nylon forms at the interface between the two layers (1)\n• Pull the nylon out with tweezers and wind it onto a rod as a continuous thread (1)` },
      { q: 'State the type of monomer used to make DNA, and name the monomers of proteins and of starch.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• DNA is made from four different nucleotides (containing adenine, thymine, cytosine and guanine) (1)\n• Proteins are made from amino acids (1)\n• Starch (and cellulose) is made from sugars (glucose) (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe and explain how crude oil is separated into fractions by fractional distillation.', marks: 6, diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): a clear description of the process with an explanation in terms of molecular size and intermolecular forces, naming fractions.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): a description with some explanation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements about heating and separating.\nIndicative content:\n• Crude oil is heated and vaporised and the vapour enters the bottom of a fractionating column (1)\n• The column is hot at the bottom and cooler at the top (temperature gradient) (1)\n• Vapours rise and condense at the level where the temperature falls below their boiling point (1)\n• Larger molecules have stronger intermolecular forces, so higher boiling points; they condense lower down (1)\n• Smaller molecules have weaker intermolecular forces and lower boiling points, so they rise higher (1)\n• Fractions named, e.g. refinery gases, petrol, kerosene, diesel, heavy fuel oil and bitumen at the bottom (1)` },
      { q: 'Most fractions from crude oil are mixtures of alkanes. Give the general formula of the alkanes and use it to write the formula of the alkane with 10 carbon atoms.', marks: 2, markScheme: `• CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ (1)\n• C₁₀H₂₂ (1)` },
      { q: 'Explain why modern life depends on crude oil and why this is a problem for the future.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Hydrocarbons from crude oil are used as fuels for transport, heating and electricity (1)\n• Crude oil is a feedstock for the petrochemical industry making plastics, solvents, medicines and detergents (1)\n• Crude oil is a finite resource which is being used up much faster than it forms (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe cracking and explain why it is carried out.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Long-chain alkanes are heated and passed over a hot catalyst (e.g. zeolite / aluminium oxide at about 500 °C) (1)\n• They break down into shorter-chain alkanes and alkenes (1)\n• There is more demand for shorter-chain fractions such as petrol than is supplied by crude oil (1)\n• The alkenes produced are used to make polymers (1)` },
      { q: 'Evaluate the use of hydrogen–oxygen fuel cells rather than rechargeable batteries to power cars. Include the overall reaction in the fuel cell.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Overall reaction: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, producing a potential difference as long as fuel is supplied (a chemical cell stops when its reactants are used up) (1)\n• Advantage: the only product is water, so there are no CO₂ emissions at the point of use (1)\n• Advantage: refuelling is quick and the range is long compared with a battery that needs recharging (1)\n• Disadvantage: hydrogen is flammable and hard to store; it is often made from fossil fuels or using electricity (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how the Earth\'s early atmosphere is thought to have formed and how an oxygen-rich atmosphere developed.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Volcanoes released gases such as carbon dioxide, water vapour, nitrogen (and small amounts of methane and ammonia) (1)\n• Water vapour condensed to form the oceans, and carbon dioxide dissolved in them (1)\n• Algae and plants evolved and produced oxygen by photosynthesis (1)\n• Carbon dioxide was removed by photosynthesis and locked into sedimentary rocks and fossil fuels, so oxygen levels increased (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the greenhouse effect in terms of the interaction of radiation with the atmosphere.', marks: 3, markScheme: `• Short-wavelength radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth\'s surface (1)\n• The Earth emits longer-wavelength infrared radiation (1)\n• Greenhouse gases such as CO₂, methane and water vapour absorb this infrared and re-emit it, warming the atmosphere (1)` },
      { q: 'Evaluate the evidence that human activity is causing climate change, and describe the uncertainties in the evidence.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Atmospheric CO₂ concentration has risen since the industrial revolution, correlating with increased burning of fossil fuels (1)\n• Global temperatures have risen alongside the rise in CO₂ (1)\n• Correlation does not prove causation; natural factors (e.g. solar activity, volcanoes) also affect climate (1)\n• Historical data are less accurate / fewer locations, and climate models are complex and rely on assumptions (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe two potential effects of increased levels of carbon dioxide and methane, and two ways these effects may be mitigated.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Effect, e.g. rising sea levels from melting ice caps and thermal expansion — flooding (1)\n• Effect, e.g. more extreme weather / changes in rainfall / changes to habitats and food production / ocean acidification (1)\n• Mitigation, e.g. use renewable energy instead of fossil fuels / improve energy efficiency (1)\n• Mitigation, e.g. carbon capture and storage / plant trees / reduce methane from livestock and landfill (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe the major sources of carbon monoxide, sulfur dioxide, oxides of nitrogen and particulates in the atmosphere and the problem caused by each.', marks: 4, markScheme: `• Carbon monoxide: incomplete combustion of fuels; toxic — combines with haemoglobin, reducing the blood\'s ability to carry oxygen (1)\n• Sulfur dioxide: burning fossil fuels containing sulfur; causes acid rain and breathing problems (1)\n• Oxides of nitrogen: nitrogen and oxygen reacting at high temperature in engines; acid rain, photochemical smog, breathing problems (1)\n• Particulates: incomplete combustion (especially diesel); global dimming and lung damage (1)` },
      { q: 'Describe how potable water is produced from (a) fresh ground water and (b) sea water, and explain why waste water is harder to treat than ground water.', marks: 5, markScheme: `• (a) Filtration through filter beds to remove solids (1)\n• (a) Sterilisation with chlorine, ozone or UV light to kill microbes (1)\n• (b) Desalination by distillation (1)\n• (b) Or by reverse osmosis using membranes; both need a lot of energy so are expensive (1)\n• Waste water contains organic matter and harmful microbes, so it needs extra stages (screening, sedimentation, aerobic and anaerobic digestion of sludge) before sterilising (1)` },
    ]
  },

};

